The Bear Call Spread is an optimal strategy when the price falls or does not change much during the period of holding. The profit zone for this strategy looks like a profit zone for the Bear Put Spread. The key difference is that with the Bear Call Spread you are immediately in profits after purchasing the strategy and don’t need to wait for a price drop.
Buying one Bear Call Spread is equal to selling an ATM call and buying an OTM call with a higher strike price (two ranges available - Narrow and Wide).

# High Volatility Options
Making profits in a volatile market no matter which direction it takes
There are two strategies that can be acquired to make profits in high volatility markets:
Straddle
Strangle
Most important thing to know about these strategies is that you are making a bet that the price of an asset will soon rise or fall significantly in either direction.
Instead of being bullish or bearish about the future price, you can have the following reasoning when buying Straddle or Strangle: “I don’t care what the price will be, but if it changes significantly in either direction during the period of holding the Straddle/Strangle, I win big.”
Key difference between the two is that Strangle is combination of OTM call + OTM put, while Straddle is combination of ATM call + ATM put. This means that Strangle is quite cheaper than Straddle.

## Straddle
High profits if the price rises or falls sharply during the period of holding

The Straddle is a strategy that helps you to make a bet on a volatility rise: that the price of an asset will soon increase or fall (any direction). The Straddle consists of a call option and a put option with the same strike price and the same expiration.
Instead of being bullish or bearish about the future price, you can have the following reasoning when buying it: “I don’t care what the price will be, but if it changes significantly in either direction during the period of holding the Straddle, I win.”
The Straddle has a limited cost and unlimited potential profit.
Buying one Straddle is equal to buying two ATM options: one ATM call and one ATM put.

## Strangle
Low cost, very high profits if the price rises or falls significantly

The Strangle is a strategy that helps you to make a bet on a volatility rise: that the price of an asset will soon rise or fall significantly in either direction. The Strangle consists of an out-of-the-money call option and an out-of-the-money put option with the same strike price and the same expiration.
Instead of being bullish or bearish about the future price, you can have the following reasoning when buying it: “I don’t care what the price will be, but if it changes significantly in either direction during the period of holding the Strangle, I win big.”
The Strangle is much cheaper than the Straddle (which consists of two at-the-money options). This means that you will pay much less when buying Strangles (two out-of-the-money options) and have a higher profits potential.
The Strangle has a limited cost and unlimited potential profit.

# Low Volatility Options
Making profits when market is trading sideways
There are two strategies that can be acquired to make profits in low volatility environment:
Long Butterfly (Inversion strategy)
Long Condor (Inversion strategy)
Most important things to know about low volatility option strategies:
Make profits when market volatility is low
Immediately in-the-money (in profits) after purchasing a strategy
P&L for low volatility one-click option strategies is distributed automatically
Keep in mind that with one-click option strategies you will have to wait until expiration to make a profit (if any). You won’t be able to change the parameters of the active strategy or exercise it before the expiration.

## Long Butterfly
Low cost, high profits if the price is about a strike price

The Long Butterfly is a strategy that helps you to make a bet on low volatility: that the price of an asset won’t rise or fall significantly in either direction during the period of holding.
Instead of being bullish or bearish about the future price, you can have the following reasoning when buying it: “I don’t care what the price will be, but if it doesn’t change significantly from current levels in either direction during the period of holding the Long Butterfly, I win.”
The Long Butterfly is an optimal strategy when the price doesn’t rise or fall during the period of holding. The profit zone for this strategy is the opposite of a Straddle: you make profits when the price doesn’t change, and you lose when there is volatility with regards to the market price at the moment of buying the Long Butterfly.
With the Long Butterfly, you have higher premiums received for ATM options you sell in comparison to the total amount of premiums for OTM options you sell when you buy the Long Condor.
Buying one Long Butterfly is equal to selling ATM Call and ATM Put and at the same time buy OTM Call and OTM Put (two ranges available - Narrow and Wide).
Long Butterfly is an inversion strategy. This means that the strategy includes selling (writing) of options, so they can't be exercised before expiry.

## Long Condor
Decent profits if the price changes slightly

The Long Condor is a strategy that helps you to make a bet on low volatility: that the price of an asset won’t rise or fall significantly in either direction during the period of holding.
Instead of being bullish or bearish about the future price, you can have the following reasoning when buying it: “I don’t care what the price will be, but if it won’t change significantly from current levels in either direction during the period of holding the Long Condor, I win.”
The Long Condor is an optimal strategy when the price is moving within the ~10% range. The profit zone for this strategy is the opposite of a Strangle: you make profits when the price moves within the 10% range, you lose money if the price moves up or down from the 10% range.
Buying one Long Condor is equal to selling OTM Call and OTM Put and buying higher OTM Call and lower OTM Put (two ranges available - Narrow and Wide).
Long Condor is an inversion strategy. This means that the strategy includes selling (writing) of options, so they can't be exercised before expiry.

# Stake & Cover Pool
Writing options in the protocol
What is Stake & Cover Pool?
Stake & Cover (S&C) is a liquidity provision and utilization model. There is no division into liquidity providers and stakers / premiums and settlement fees. Instead of many different liquidity pools and staking contracts, there is only one Stake & Cover pool. Staked tokens are utilized to cover the protocol's net losses on selling options/strategies and earn net profits on all expired options/strategies.
With Stake & Cover, token holders can provide tokens into the pool that will be used for collateralizing options and option strategies acquired through the protocol. The Stake & Cover (S&C) Pool participants receive 100% of net premiums earned on selling ATM & OTM options (both ETH and WBTC calls & puts) and options strategies (all of them). The net premiums earned (or losses accrued) are distributed pro-rata among all of the S&C Pool participants.
The net premium is a difference between the total premiums received from all options/strategies buyers and the total profits paid out to options/strategies holders who exercised their contracts in-the-money.
How is P&L distributed among the S&C participants?
The accumulation of P&L is conducted during a set period called an Epoch.
If at the end of an Epoch the P&L is positive, 100% of it then distributed among the Stake & Cover pool participants in USDm with regards to their shares in the S&C (pro rata distribution).
If at the end of an Epoch the P&L is negative, then a pro rata share of tokens from S&C will be converted into USDm for covering the net negative difference.
The conversion rate is publicly announced five days in advance before a new Epoch starts. The initial USDm liquidity for converting tokens into for covering the potential negative P&L will be provided by the Development Fund.
How do I get USDm if P&L was positive at the end of an Epoch?
If you were participating with tokens in Stake & Cover during the Epoch that had positive P&L accrued, you will be able to claim your share of USDm at any moment after the end of the Epoch.
This share of P&L in USDm is linked to your address and won't be utilized in the next Epochs. If the following Epoch(s) will result in negative P&L accrued, it will be covered by your share of tokens allocated in Stake & Cover, not by the unclaimed USDm balance.
How is the token/USDm conversion rate determined for covering the negative P&L?
The protocol is using experimental token price monitoring mechanics that are used for setting the conversion rate based on the token price performance during the previous Epoch.
If you don't agree to participate in the S&C Epoch with a set token/USDm conversion rate, you can request to not participate in the following Epoch and tokens will be sent to your address at the end of the current Epoch. Otherwise, your tokens will be automatically utilized in the following Epoch.
Is it possible that Epoch loss is more than S&C pool can handle?
There are risk management measures in place that are directly to connected to how much options can be sold in regards to amount of tokens staked in S&C pool, and how much of each type can be sold - amount of available bullish/bearish options, and low/high volatility options should always be in balance.
How long is one Epoch?
The total duration of an Epoch is 45 days. After the end of one Epoch, the next begins immediately. Epoch durations can be changed in order to protect S&C participants from sharply increased volatility.
Tokens can be allocated in Stake & Cover only in the first 7 days of every Epoch. The token/USDm conversion rate for the next Epoch is decided five days before the end of ongoing Epoch.
A request for withdrawing tokens from Stake & Cover can be sent at any moment during the Epoch. The withdrawal will be automatically conducted at the end of an Epoch.
How do I know if P&L is positive or negative during the Epoch?
You can find and monitor the current P&L of the operational pool in real-time on the Stake & Cover page of the website.
This metric might be changing during the whole period of an Epoch as new options and option strategies will expire or will be exercised by the users. The final P&L (positive or negative) for settlement is automatically set at the moment of an Epoch’s end.
You can follow and verify the P&L numbers independently following the operational pool’s on-chain balance.
Can I have a look at the past performance of Stake & Cover?
You can find the historical data on P&L behind Stake & Cover previous Epochs looking at the graph on the Stake & Cover page of the website. Note that past performance is no guarantee of future results.
Example of both positive and negative P&L at the end of a Epoch
Let's assume that at the beginning of the Epoch, fixed token price is $0.02, user BigOptionPlaya staked 500,000 tokens (10,000 USD worth), and the total amount of staked tokens for that Epoch was 100,000,000 (1 million USD worth).
Positive P&L example:
Total revenue from selling options was 500,000 USDm
Profits that were paid out to option buyers who exercised their options in-the-money were 300,000 USDm
That means Net Premium of the Epoch was 200,000 USDm
Net P&L per token therefore is equal to Net Premium/Amount of staked tokens, or in our example 200,000/10,000,000 = $0.002 per token
User BigOptionPlaya profit is equal to his amount of staked tokens * Profit per token, or as in our example, 500,000 * $0.002 = $1,000
As user's token stack was worth $10,000 at the time of staking, his profit during Epoch was exactly 10%, or if we convert it into an APR, it results in a +120% APR
Negative P&L example:
Total revenue from selling options was 500,000 USDm
Profits that were paid out to option buyers who exercised their options in-the-money was 600,000 USDm
That means Net Premium of the Epoch was negative 100,000 USDm, so Epoch ended in a Net loss of 100,000 USDm
Loss per token therefore is equal to Net Loss/Amount of staked tokens, or as in our example, 100,000/10,000,000 = $0.001 per token
User BigOptionPlaya loss is equal to his amount of staked tokens * loss per token, or in our example 500,000 * $0.001 = $500
As fixed token price before Epoch started was $0.02, the amount of tokens the user will lose is equal to $500/$0.02 = 25,000 tokens
User's stack at the end of the epoch will be lowered by 25,000 tokens, so he will have 475,000 tokens, therefore the realized loss is 5% of staked tokens.

